## Traditional ublk mapping versus zero-copy features

drivers/block/ublk_drv.c:1419-1493 uses import_ubuf and copy iter helpers for
traditional ublk request/server-buffer transfers. This path does not require
pinning the server mapping with GUP. Passing the CPU mapping of a dma-heap
buffer as the traditional server address is therefore plausible; it is not a
tested implementation. Preserve DMA CPU-access synchronization around ublk's
kernel CPU copy, not merely around Rust slice access.

SHMEM_ZC is a different path. It pins registered buffers with FOLL_LONGTERM
(ublk_drv.c:5467) and matches request PFNs; ordinary buffered application I/O
does not share those pages. System/CMA dma-heap mappings are PFN mappings, so
do not assume they are GUP-compatible or suitable SHMEM_ZC registrations.
memfd plus udmabuf is a separate possible experiment, not a validated bridge.
SHMEM descriptors encode a buffer index plus offset; FunctionFS's DMA transfer
does not accept offsets. A single large arena cannot simply be passed through.

Fixed-kernel-buffer zero-copy / AUTO_BUF_REG exposes request bvecs to compatible
io_uring operations. Normal FunctionFS read_iter/write_iter still allocates
staging memory and invokes copy_from_iter/copy_to_iter (f_fs.c allocation and
ffs_user_copy_worker paths). Merely switching the operation to fixed buffers
does not make the USB leg zero-copy.

## DMA-BUF submission is not a reliable per-transfer result API

include/uapi/linux/usb/functionfs.h defines the DMA transfer as fd, flags and
length; there is no offset or per-transfer result/actual-length output.
ffs_dmabuf_attach rejects non-SG gadget controllers (f_fs.c:1544-1545).
ffs_dmabuf_find_attachment returns -EPERM if not attached (:1529).
Transfer queues usb_request SG entries from the DMA attachment. The function
documents usb_ep_queue FIFO order, allowing distinct buffers to be submitted
in protocol order before previous completions are reaped.

Three independent completion problems matter for smoo:

1. ffs_epfile_dmabuf_io_complete at f_fs.c:1464-1469 passes only req->status
   to ffs_dmabuf_signal_done. The latter stores status in fence->error then
   signals (:1444-1453). req->actual is not exported. A successful short OUT
   transfer is not distinguishable from a full transfer by this DMA UAPI.
2. sync_file_poll at drivers/dma-buf/sync_file.c:197-210 returns EPOLLIN for any
   signaled fence, irrespective of its error. POLLIN is completion, not success.
   SYNC_IOC_FILE_INFO can retrieve status for a fence actually captured.
3. Export after submission is only a snapshot of pending work and can lose an
   already-completed error. dma-buf.c:458-464 calls dma_resv_get_singleton and
   substitutes dma_fence_get_stub if none. dma-resv.c:627-641 calls get_fences;
   get_fences uses unlocked iteration (:578-579); the iterator skips signaled
   fences (:402-404). Thus a fast failed USB completion before export can become
   a success stub. Querying FILE_INFO after export alone does not close the race.

These source facts undermine a claim that fence waiting alone establishes
successful delivery of exactly the requested bytes. A future prototype needs
fault injection and potentially kernel API work to obtain a stable transfer
result and actual length. No maintainer discussion or proposed patch was
reviewed in this session; do that prior-art work when pursuing a kernel change.

## Cache and lifetime constraints

include/uapi/linux/dma-buf.h:29-47 requires mapped CPU access between SYNC START
and END, with the appropriate read/write flags. Cache management is separate
from exclusion: no CPU or tag reuse while USB owns that memory. Both endpoint
attachments can exist, but the same buffer must not be submitted concurrently
to both directions. Reattach when endpoint generation changes; retain storage,
attachments and owned fds until cancellation/completion has been reaped.
Do not infer that aborting a userspace async task cancels its blocking ioctl.

Dual endpoint attachment also needs a targeted coherency test: f_fs maps IN
DMA_TO_DEVICE and OUT DMA_FROM_DEVICE (f_fs.c:1563), while system heap CPU
access callbacks walk all mapped attachments using the caller's sync direction
(drivers/dma-buf/heaps/system_heap.c:186-225). DMA debug diagnoses a sync
direction differing from a non-bidirectional mapping (kernel/dma/debug.c:1176-1186).
Do not presume that adjacent sync calls or changing everything to SYNC_RW solves
this. Test with DMA_API_DEBUG and a noncoherent target; mapping policy remains
part of the prototype rather than an established design property.

